Here is a basic and simple Number Guessing Game using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. It has a uniform design, and all three parts are easy to understand.

## 1. Folder structure

Create a folder named `NumberGame` with these three files:

```
NumberGame/
├── index.html
├── style.css
└── script.js
```

## 2. HTML — `index.html`

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Number Guessing Game</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

    <div class="game">
        <h1>Number Guessing Game</h1>
        <p>Guess a number between 1 and 10</p>

        <input type="number" id="guess" placeholder="Enter number">

        <button onclick="checkGuess()">Guess</button>

        <p id="result"></p>

        <button onclick="resetGame()">Reset</button>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

## 3. CSS — `style.css`

```
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f0f4f8;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    height: 100vh;
}

.game {
    background-color: white;
    padding: 30px;
    text-align: center;
    border-radius: 10px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px gray;
}

input {
    padding: 10px;
    width: 150px;
    margin: 10px;
}

button {
    padding: 10px 15px;
    background-color: #007bff;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    cursor: pointer;
}

button:hover {
    background-color: #0056b3;
}

#result {
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

## 4. JavaScript — `script.js`

```
let number = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10) + 1;

function checkGuess() {
    let guess = Number(document.getElementById("guess").value);
    let result = document.getElementById("result");

    if (guess < 1 || guess > 10 || !guess) {
        result.innerHTML = "Enter a number from 1 to 10!";
    }
    else if (guess === number) {
        result.innerHTML = "Correct! You won!";
    }
    else if (guess < number) {
        result.innerHTML = "Too low! Try again.";
    }
    else {
        result.innerHTML = "Too high! Try again.";
    }
}

function resetGame() {
    number = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10) + 1;
    document.getElementById("guess").value = "";
    document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "";
}
```

## 5. How to run

1. Create the three files in the same folder.
2. Copy the respective code into each file.
3. Save all files.
4. Double-click `index.html` to open it in your browser.
5. Enter a number between 1 and 10 and click Guess.

## 6. Important concepts

| Technology         | Purpose                                         |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |
| HTML               | Creates the input, buttons, and headings.       |
| CSS                | Adds colors, spacing, and layout.               |
| JavaScript         | Generates a random number and checks the guess. |
| `getElementById()` | Accesses an HTML element.                       |
| `Math.random()`    | Generates a random decimal.                     |
| `onclick`          | Calls a function when a button is clicked.      |

Game logic: The computer selects a random number from 1 to 10. You win when your guess matches that number. Click Reset to start a new game.






















## 1. Folder structure

Create a folder named `MoleRatGame` with these three files:

```
MoleRatGame/
├── index.html
├── style.css
└── script.js
```

## 2. HTML — `index.html`

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Whack-a-Mole Game</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

    <h1>🐹 Whack-a-Mole Game</h1>
    <h2>Score: <span id="score">0</span></h2>

    <div class="game">
        <button onclick="hitMole(0)" id="hole0">🐹</button>
        <button onclick="hitMole(1)" id="hole1">🐹</button>
        <button onclick="hitMole(2)" id="hole2">🐹</button>
        <button onclick="hitMole(3)" id="hole3">🐹</button>
        <button onclick="hitMole(4)" id="hole4">🐹</button>
        <button onclick="hitMole(5)" id="hole5">🐹</button>
    </div>

    <p id="message">Click the mole to score!</p>

    <button onclick="startGame()">Start Game</button>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

## 3. CSS — `style.css`

```
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    text-align: center;
    background-color: #e8f5e9;
}

h1 {
    color: #2e7d32;
}

.game {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 100px);
    gap: 15px;
    justify-content: center;
    margin: 25px;
}

.game button {
    height: 100px;
    width: 100px;
    font-size: 40px;
    background-color: #a5d6a7;
    border: 3px solid #388e3c;
    border-radius: 50%;
    cursor: pointer;
}

button {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    background-color: #2e7d32;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    cursor: pointer;
}

#message {
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

## 4. JavaScript — `script.js`

```
let score = 0;
let mole = -1;
let timer;

function showMole() {
    for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        document.getElementById("hole" + i).innerHTML = "🕳️";
    }

    mole = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6);

    document.getElementById("hole" + mole).innerHTML = "🐹";
}

function hitMole(id) {
    if (id === mole) {
        score++;
        document.getElementById("score").innerHTML = score;
        document.getElementById("message").innerHTML = "Good job!";
        showMole();
    } else {
        document.getElementById("message").innerHTML = "Try again!";
    }
}

function startGame() {
    clearInterval(timer);

    score = 0;
    document.getElementById("score").innerHTML = score;
    document.getElementById("message").innerHTML = "Catch the mole!";

    showMole();

    timer = setInterval(showMole, 1000);
}
```

## 5. How to run

1. Create the three files in the same folder.
2. Paste each code into its respective file and save.
3. Open `index.html` in Chrome.
4. Click Start Game.
5. Click the 🐹 mole before it moves to another hole.

## 6. How it works

| Concept           | Purpose                                      |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| HTML              | Creates the game holes and score display.    |
| CSS               | Arranges the holes in a 3 × 2 grid.          |
| `Math.random()`   | Selects a random hole.                       |
| `hitMole()`       | Checks whether the correct hole was clicked. |
| `setInterval()`   | Moves the mole every second.                 |
| `clearInterval()` | Clears the previous timer when restarting.   |

Note: This basic version has no time limit. The mole keeps moving until you stop playing or close the page.







Here is a simple Form Submission and Display program using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. When you submit the form, the entered details will be displayed below it without refreshing the page.

## 1. Folder structure

```
FormApp/
├── index.html
├── style.css
└── script.js
```

## 2. HTML — `index.html`

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Student Form</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

    <div class="container">
        <h2>Student Registration</h2>

        <form id="myForm">
            <input type="text" id="name" placeholder="Enter Name" required>
            <input type="email" id="email" placeholder="Enter Email" required>
            <input type="text" id="course" placeholder="Enter Course" required>

            <button type="submit">Submit</button>
        </form>

        <div id="output"></div>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

## 3. CSS — `style.css`

```
body {
    font-family: Arial;
    background-color: #eaf2f8;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-top: 50px;
}

.container {
    background: white;
    padding: 25px;
    width: 300px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px gray;
}

h2 {
    text-align: center;
    color: #007bff;
}

input {
    width: 95%;
    padding: 10px 0;
    margin: 8px 0;
}

button {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 10px;
    background: #007bff;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
}

#output {
    margin-top: 20px;
    color: green;
}
```

## 4. JavaScript — `script.js`

```
document.getElementById("myForm").addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    let name = document.getElementById("name").value;
    let email = document.getElementById("email").value;
    let course = document.getElementById("course").value;

    document.getElementById("output").innerHTML =
        "<h3>Submitted Details</h3>" +
        "<p>Name: " + name + "</p>" +
        "<p>Email: " + email + "</p>" +
        "<p>Course: " + course + "</p>";

    document.getElementById("myForm").reset();
});
```

## 5. Sample output

## Student Registration

NamePriya

Emailpriya\@gmail.com

CourseComputer Science

### Submitted Details

Name: Priya

Email: priya\@gmail.com

Course: Computer Science

This illustrates how the submitted details appear below the form.

## 6. Important concepts

| Code                              | Purpose                                     |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| `form`                            | Collects user input.                        |
| `required`                        | Prevents submission when a field is empty.  |
| `addEventListener("submit", ...)` | Runs JavaScript when the form is submitted. |
| `event.preventDefault()`          | Prevents the page from refreshing.          |
| `.value`                          | Gets the entered input.                     |
| `innerHTML`                       | Displays the submitted details.             |
| `reset()`                         | Clears the form fields.                     |

How to run: Save all three files in the same folder and open `index.html` in your browser. Fill in the details and click Submit. The submitted information will appear below the form.
